ONNX Runtime (pinned ORT_VERSION): replace ROCm's small-K TopK dispatch with
a self-contained kernel, avoiding a hipCUB block-primitive tie-breaking defect
that picks a different winner among exactly-tied candidates from run to run
on bit-identical input.

WHY
---
onnxruntime/core/providers/cuda/math/topk_impl.cuh implements the generic
ONNX TopK kernel three ways depending on shape: BitonicTopK (small enough to
fit one block, no external dependency), RadixTopK (moderate K, built on
hipCUB's BlockScan/BlockReduce/BlockRadixSort -- rocPRIM-backed on ROCm), and
a cub::DeviceRadixSort-based fallback for large K. This file is hipified
verbatim from the CUDA provider (no ROCm-specific source exists), so ROCm
gets NVIDIA's algorithm running on AMD's own reimplementation of the cub API
surface.

BeamSearchTest.GptBeamSearchFp32_DisableFastTopK (which forces ORT's generic
TopK fallback via ORT_BEAM_SEARCH_USE_FAST_TOPK=0, bypassing the specialized
beam-search topk kernel) failed roughly half of isolated single-test runs --
not under load, alone, fresh process each time. Traced to RadixTopK: this
test's toy GPT2 model outputs literal 0.0 logits at several decode steps,
producing heavy exact ties among top-K candidates. RadixTopK's own tie
handling (an `equal_quota` scheme built from BlockReduce/BlockScan exclusive
prefix sums, meant to deterministically allocate tied slots by thread index)
picked a different winner among tied candidates across runs on the same
input. Confirmed via a non-invasive host-side dump (deliberately added no new
synchronization or device-to-host copy beyond what already existed in the
calling code, since an earlier, more invasive dump masked the failure
entirely by changing kernel launch timing -- the same observer effect already
documented for the gfx803 small-GEMM race): printed values were exact `0`
at 9 significant digits, not near-zero floating-point noise, ruling out
upstream numeric nondeterminism as the cause.

Two more targeted fixes were tried and rejected:

- Routing to cub::DeviceRadixSort (this file's own fallback for large K, a
  different hipCUB code path -- device-wide, not block-level primitives)
  segfaults. Confirmed via backtrace: it crashes in
  RocmKernel::GetScratchBuffer, because the beam-search call site
  (onnxruntime/contrib_ops/cuda/transformers/generation_device_helper.cc)
  deliberately passes `kernel=nullptr` to TopKImpl, with an inline comment
  explaining why: "We limit number of beams in BeamSearchParameters, so
  K <= 256 and use NULL here" -- banking on always landing in RadixTopK,
  never the kernel-dependent DeviceRadixSort path.
- Extending BitonicTopK's applicability (no hipCUB dependency at all) beyond
  its current `aligned_dimension <= blockDim` gate isn't a safe drop-in: its
  sort/merge phases index via tid/(tid<<1) pairs that only cover positions
  reachable within one block's thread count in a single pass. Making it work
  for this test's dimension (4000) would mean rewriting the compare-exchange
  network to handle multiple elements per thread throughout -- a much larger,
  correctness-sensitive change than scoped here.

WHAT
----
Added SafeSmallKTopK: K sequential block-wide argmax passes, entirely
hand-written (no hipCUB block primitives at all, so no dependency on
rocPRIM's BlockScan/BlockReduce/BlockRadixSort), no CudaKernel dependency (so
it works with kernel=nullptr), and no O(dimension) shared memory -- the
"already selected" check scans the K selected indices found so far each pass
(K is small by construction; this kernel only replaces RadixTopK, gated the
same way the original code already gated it: K <= BT*16 or unsorted output),
not a dimension-sized flag array. Shared memory scales with block size and K
only, so it stays cheap for the vocab sizes real beam search sees, unlike a
naive "mark every taken index" approach would.

Ties are broken by lowest index, matching this file's existing
IS_SMALLER/BIGGER macros' tie-break convention (see their `.val` clause) --
deterministic and reproducible given identical input, unlike RadixTopK's.

Scoped to ROCm only via #ifdef USE_ROCM (the standard guard already used
throughout ORT's shared cuda/ provider files for exactly this kind of
ROCm-only behavior difference) -- CUDA is untouched, since RadixTopK isn't
broken there.

Verified: BeamSearchTest.GptBeamSearchFp32_DisableFastTopK, 18 isolated runs,
17 passed (the one residual failure showed the identical symptom -- likely a
separate, deeper source, not chased further here; see
gfx803/KERNEL_BUGS.md). Full TopKOperator suite (53 tests, including
Top3AllSame -- an explicit tie-handling test -- and large-array cases) plus
BeamSearchTest/SamplingTest: 62 passed, 0 regressions.

Applies to: ORT's ROCm EP generally (the hipCUB block-primitive tie-break
issue isn't gfx803-specific -- AMD's 64-wide wavefront vs NVIDIA's 32-wide
warp is uniform across ROCm architectures), though only verified on gfx803
here. See topk-radix-tiebreak-nondeterministic.sh for how this patch is
applied and verified at build time.

